<TextPage title="Editorial Standards" seoTitle="Editorial Standards | Surf and Dive" description="How Surf and Dive researches, writes, scores and updates its luxury surf, dive and ocean travel guides, and what we will never publish." eyebrow="Trust" lede="The standards behind every destination guide, comparison and article on Surf and Dive." path="/editorial-standards/">
  <h2>Sources and verification</h2>
  <p>We ground factual claims in first-party tourism boards, reputable surf, dive and freediving organisations and established travel sources. Blog posts that rely on a specific official source carry <strong>source notes</strong>. High-risk claims (access, regulations, marine conditions and safety) are written cautiously and marked for verification before travel.</p>

  <h2>Opinion and fact</h2>
  <p>We separate editorial opinion from objective information. Phrases such as “our verdict”, “we would prioritise” and “generally favours” signal editorial judgement. Descriptions of seasons use qualified language (<em>generally, typically, often, depending on region</em>) because conditions vary.</p>

  <h2>Scoring methodology</h2>
  <p>Each destination receives five editorial positioning scores from 1 to 10: surf, diving, freediving, luxury and scenery. They reflect the overall quality and range of experiences available to a traveller with appropriate ability, in the right season, with a good operator. They are comparison aids, not scientific rankings, and not a promise about any particular day, site or property.</p>

  <h2>What we never publish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fabricated first-hand experiences, reviews, testimonials or expert quotes.</li>
    <li>Invented prices, availability, flight times, awards, credentials or partnerships.</li>
    <li>Guarantees of waves, visibility, weather or wildlife encounters.</li>
    <li>Advice that encourages unsafe behaviour, including solo freediving, breath-hold competition, entering conditions beyond your ability, or touching coral and wildlife.</li>
    <li>Photography that misrepresents a real place. Destination imagery must be authentic and licensed; we never use AI-generated images to depict real resorts, reefs, waves or wildlife encounters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Photography</h2>
  <p>Where photography has not yet been commissioned or licensed, our pages show clearly labelled image slots rather than generic stock imagery. Alt text always describes the actual photograph.</p>

  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>Blog posts show published and updated dates. Seasonal and operational details are reviewed regularly; where information changes frequently we say “check before travel” rather than presenting an old fact as permanent. If you spot an error, please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. We correct promptly and note significant changes.</p>

  <h2>Translations</h2>
  <p>We launch in English. Localised editions will only be published once written or reviewed by native-language editors.</p>
